#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106858} :activity/id #uuid "223dc57a-1055-5f50-87c7-e51875351e53" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106858 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106994 :tutorial/content "Recall that the distance between vertices $u$ and $v$ in an unweighted undirected graph, $d(u,v)$, is the length (i.e., the number of edges) of the shortest path from $u$ to $v$. If no path from $u$ to $v$ exists, we say that the distance is $\\infty$.\n\nIn a simple undirected unweighted graph $G=(V,E)$ with $n$ vertices $v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n$, the **distance matrix** $D$ of the graph is an $n \\times n$ matrix (table), where the entry $a_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column equals the *distance* between the vertices $v_i$ and $v_j$.\n\nNotice that, according to this definition:\n\n- If no path exists between $v_i$ and $v_j$, then the entry $a_{ij}$ is $\\infty$.\n- If $i=j$ (i.e., it is the same vertex), then the entry $a_{ii}$ is $0$.\n- The distance matrix of an undirected graph is symmetric about the main diagonal.\n\nLet's look at an example of how to build the distance matrix for the graph below.\n\n![](images/ef/efa72276c05169b41b045d91557a774569fe7ef6271d1a1dd9546cd339e98a41.png)\n\nWe start with a square $4 \\times 4$ matrix with $0$s on the main diagonal and examine every pair of vertices to determine the remaining matrix entries.\n\n- The path $v_2-v_3-v_4$ has two edges and is the shortest path between $v_2$ and $v_4$. Hence, we write $2$ in the entry $a_{24}$ and, by symmetry, in the entry $a_{42}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ |  |  |  |\n| $v_2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| $v_3$ |  |  | $0$ |  |\n| $v_4$ |  | $2$ |  | $0$ |\n\n- Since the vertices $v_2$ and $v_3$ are adjacent, the shortest path between them is $1$. Hence, we write $1$ in the entry $a_{23}$ and, by symmetry, in the entry $a_{32}$. Similarly, since $v_3$ and $v_4$ are adjacent, we write $1$ in the entry $a_{34}$ and $a_{43}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ |  |  |  |\n| $v_2$ |  | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $v_3$ |  | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $v_4$ |  | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\n- Since the vertex $v_1$ is isolated, the distance from it to any other vertex is $\\infty$. Hence, we write $\\infty$ in every entry of the $1$st row and in every entry of the $1$st column, except for $a_{11}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ |\n| $v_2$ | $\\infty$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $v_3$ | $\\infty$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $v_4$ | $\\infty$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nThe distance matrix is completed. In regular matrix notation, we can write this distance matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & ∞ & ∞ & ∞ \\\\ ∞ & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ ∞ & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ ∞ & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2193f964-a8a3-5cbd-915c-6462ea48cf94" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10215 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4a6d51b8-f1b3-53b8-a533-260a70384816" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106859}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106859 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105873 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "faf23acf-d81c-5437-9842-6d176449a5a9"} :step/id #uuid "9b95db70-c6cf-5a04-bc0a-4535d25a40e2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106860}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106860 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107023 :tutorial/content "Recall that the distance between vertices $u$ and $v$ in an unweighted directed graph, $d(u,v)$, is the length (i.e., the number of edges) of the shortest path from $u$ to $v$. If no path from $u$ to $v$ exists, we say that the distance is $\\infty$.\n\nIn a simple directed unweighted graph $G=(V,E)$ with $n$ vertices $v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n$, the **distance matrix** $D$ of the graph is an $n \\times n$ matrix, where the entry $a_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column represents the *distance* between the vertices $v_i$ and $v_j$.\n\nNotice that, according to this definition:\n\n- If no path exists between $v_i$ and $v_j$, then the entry $a_{ij}$ is $\\infty$.\n- If $i=j$ (i.e., it is the same vertex), then the entry $a_{ii}$ is $0$.\n- The distance matrix of a directed graph is *not* symmetric about the main diagonal in general.\n\nLet's look at an example of how to build the distance matrix for the graph below.\n\n![](images/6e/6e105ae2ce9cbdd8f4494b9a0356f136da06f60e93250c45348811b3bc8ce4cc.png)\n\nWe start with a square $4 \\times 4$ matrix with $0$s on the main diagonal and examine every pair of vertices to determine the remaining matrix entries.\n\n- The path $v_1 \\to v_2 \\to v_3 \\to v_4$ has three edges and is the shortest path between $v_1$ and $v_4$. Hence, we write $3$ in the entry $a_{14}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ |  |  | $3$ |\n| $v_2$ |  | $0$ |  |  |\n| $v_3$ |  |  | $0$ |  |\n| $v_4$ |  |  |  | $0$ |\n\n- Similarly, the path $v_2 \\to v_3 \\to v_4$ has two edges and is the shortest path between $v_2$ and $v_4$. Hence, we write $2$ in the entry $a_{24}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ |  |  | $3$ |\n| $v_2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| $v_3$ |  |  | $0$ |  |\n| $v_4$ |  |  |  | $0$ |\n\n- Similarly, the path $v_4 \\to v_2 \\to v_3$ has two edges and is the shortest path between $v_4$ and $v_3$. Hence, we write $2$ in the entry $a_{43}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ |  |  | $3$ |\n| $v_2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| $v_3$ |  |  | $0$ |  |\n| $v_4$ |  |  | $2$ | $0$ |\n\n- We write $1$ in every entry that represents a vertex pair connected by a direct edge: $a_{12}, a_{23}, a_{34}$, and $a_{42}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| $v_2$ |  | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $v_3$ |  |  | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $v_4$ |  | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\n- Finally, we write $\\infty$ in all other empty cells to indicate that no paths exist between the corresponding vertex pairs.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ | $3$ |\n| $v_2$ | $\\infty$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $v_3$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $v_4$ | $\\infty$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nThe distance matrix is completed. In regular matrix notation, we can write this distance matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & ∞ & 3 \\\\ ∞ & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ ∞ & ∞ & 0 & 1 \\\\ ∞ & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b87ff0c3-989b-5bbd-9a38-698f26547311" :tutorial/math-academy-id 11606 :tutorial/title "Distance Matrix of a Directed Graph"} :step/id #uuid "b28a5462-157d-5a73-ae70-78de6ba43d21" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106861}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106861 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ebcfd62-99c4-5fd5-8ebb-94e2e0c71bd3"} :step/id #uuid "5a7b2883-200f-536c-acb2-482a4a9a22ca" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106862}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106862 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107024 :tutorial/content "Recall that the distance between vertices $u$ and $v$ in a weighted graph, $d(u,v)$, is the length (i.e., sum of the weights of edges) of the shortest path from $u$ to $v$. If no path from $u$ to $v$ exists, we say that the distance is $\\infty$.\n\nIn a weighted graph $G=(V,E)$ with $n$ vertices $v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n$, the **distance matrix** $D$ of the graph is an $n \\times n$ matrix, where the entry $a_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column equals the *distance* between the vertices $v_i$ and $v_j$. Here $G$ can be either directed or undirected.\n\nNotice that, according to this definition:\n\n- If no path exists between $v_i$ and $v_j$, then the entry $a_{ij}$ is $\\infty$.\n- If $i=j$ (i.e., it is the same vertex), then the entry $a_{ii}$ is $0$.\n- The distance matrix of an undirected weighted graph is symmetric about the main diagonal, but the distance matrix of a directed weighted graph is *not* symmetric in general.\n\nLet's look at an example of how to build the distance matrix for the directed weighted graph below.\n\n![](images/45/457cc5d27f10b40f82a6951b3d88964ca9455530adde7fcd8abb797f0f16fd32.png)\n\nWe start with a square $4 \\times 4$ matrix with $0$s on the main diagonal and examine every pair of vertices to determine the remaining matrix entries.\n\n- The detour path $v_1 \\to v_2 \\to v_3 \\to v_4$ has a weight of $2+1+5=8$ and is the shortest path between $v_1$ and $v_4$. (Notice that the alternative path $v_1 \\to v_2 \\to v_4$, which has fewer edges, has a greater weight of $2+7=9$.) Hence, we write $8$ in the entry $a_{14}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ |  |  | $8$ |\n| $v_2$ |  | $0$ |  |  |\n| $v_3$ |  |  | $0$ |  |\n| $v_4$ |  |  |  | $0$ |\n\n- Similarly, the path $v_2 \\to v_3 \\to v_4$ has a weight of $1+5=6$ and is the shortest path between $v_2$ and $v_4$. Hence, we write $6$ in the entry $a_{24}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ |  |  | $8$ |\n| $v_2$ |  | $0$ |  | $6$ |\n| $v_3$ |  |  | $0$ |  |\n| $v_4$ |  |  |  | $0$ |\n\n- We write the weight of an edge in the entry corresponding to a vertex pair connected by a direct edge with no shorter detours exist: $a_{12}, a_{23}$, and $a_{34}$.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ | $2$ |  | $8$ |\n| $v_2$ |  | $0$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n| $v_3$ |  |  | $0$ | $5$ |\n| $v_4$ |  |  |  | $0$ |\n\n- Finally, we write $\\infty$ in all other empty cells to indicate that no paths exist between the corresponding vertex pairs.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $\\infty$ | $8$ |\n| $v_2$ | $\\infty$ | $0$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n| $v_3$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $0$ | $5$ |\n| $v_4$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $0$ |\n\nThe distance matrix is completed. In regular matrix notation, we can write this distance matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & ∞ & 8 \\\\ ∞ & 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ ∞ & ∞ & 0 & 5 \\\\ ∞ & ∞ & ∞ & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5c48d96b-6fee-5403-a657-3f623317d825" :tutorial/math-academy-id 11607 :tutorial/title "Distance Matrix of a Weighted Graph"} :step/id #uuid "58ab1fbd-2cff-5eb4-bcde-002f27a8ef18" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106863}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106863 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e142426-f58b-58c2-9807-aba3e0aeb40a"} :step/id #uuid "42aab47b-85f4-5d78-9ec5-d86224e66683"}] :activity/title "Distance Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106012}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186050837 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105873 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105499 :question/id #uuid "953b8bd8-63f6-5802-9ef3-4292ab295db6" :question/math-academy-id "e-16002" :question/problem "Consider the following disconnected graph.\n\n![](images/92/92d32d3ff97b9602be4c2ae10a778be395e749ae91d5be819a0e4953b8f96de6.png)\n\nFill in the missing values in the following distance matrix for the graph above.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ | $v_5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ |  | $1$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |  |\n| $v_2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ |  | $1$ |\n| $v_3$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ | $1$ |\n| $v_4$ | $\\infty$ |  | $\\infty$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ |\n| $v_5$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ | $0$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a fixed order of vertices $v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n$ in an undirected graph, the *distance matrix* $D$ of the graph is an $n \\times n$-matrix (table), where the element $d_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column equals the distance between the vertices $v_i$ and $v_j$.\n\nIf there is no path form $v_i$ and $v_j$, we define\n\n$$\nd_{ij}=\\infty\n$$\n\nThe distances are calculated as follows:\n\n- First, the entry $d_{11}$ is the distance from $v_1$ to itself, which is always zero. Hence,\n$d_{11} = 0$.\n- Now, recall that the distance matrix of an undirected graph is symmetric about the main diagonal. Hence, by symmetry, we have\n$d_{15} = d_{51} = 2$.\n- Finally, the distance from $v_2$ to $v_4$ is $\\infty$ because there is no path from $v_2$ to $v_4$. Hence, we have\n$d_{24} = d_{42} = \\infty$.\n\nTherefore, the distance matrix is as follows:\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ | $v_5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$ |\n| $v_2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\infty}$ | $1$ |\n| $v_3$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ | $1$ |\n| $v_4$ | $\\infty$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\infty}$ | $\\infty$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ |\n| $v_5$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ | $0$ |"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "faf23acf-d81c-5437-9842-6d176449a5a9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106224 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "a911d014-741a-5544-9d94-b8878d7da9af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103403} {:answer-field/id #uuid "bc5ec242-ac80-54f5-996d-552df1c55712" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103404} {:answer-field/id #uuid "0d5baf53-2813-5680-8e12-b7971c25b3df" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103405} {:answer-field/id #uuid "11bb0f4f-90bd-5bdb-b11d-5992ddc5727c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103406} {:answer-field/id #uuid "715428f8-9cb8-54a3-bc0b-25d49736fe98" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103407}] :question/id #uuid "6640fd47-e8a6-5e73-8678-64a596328d81" :question/math-academy-id "q-243217" :question/problem "![](images/2e/2ea96fe9ca41d9f840bc84d243cd61a12c3d35a59305f69e6625b2dd45da2ad6.png)\n\nFill in the missing values in the distance matrix for the graph above:\n\n|  | $v_{1}$ | $v_{2}$ | $v_{3}$ | $v_{4}$ | $v_{5}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_{1}$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} | $1$ | {{field-2}} | $3$ |\n| $v_{2}$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $v_{3}$ | $1$ | $1$ | {{field-4}} | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $v_{4}$ | $2$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $v_{5}$ | $3$ | {{field-5}} | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "a044f441-6a1b-5926-8e1d-44154ef98d82" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103671} {:answer-field/id #uuid "a3aeaa7b-5e7e-5f58-afe9-9e90dd634ba6" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103672} {:answer-field/id #uuid "c6366363-9b44-519f-9fb9-edb398bbc57f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103673} {:answer-field/id #uuid "1c1f2e25-1f1c-5ea1-840d-715856ee40e6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103674} {:answer-field/id #uuid "7b5cd8ad-e922-5289-979c-72435332c46a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103675}] :question/id #uuid "2446ac11-949f-57b5-b036-599f15f3f512" :question/math-academy-id "q-257951" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d36ad4e9a0f221d36e96d13f70ae57fe40ae55ad2763b7308aad6994bc942e44.png)\n\nFill in the missing values in the distance matrix for the graph above:\n\n|  | $v_{1}$ | $v_{2}$ | $v_{3}$ | $v_{4}$ | $v_{5}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_{1}$ | $0$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} | $∞$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $v_{2}$ | $1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $∞$ | $∞$ |\n| $v_{3}$ | {{field-3}} | $2$ | $0$ | $∞$ | $∞$ |\n| $v_{4}$ | $∞$ | $∞$ | $∞$ | {{field-4}} | $1$ |\n| $v_{5}$ | $∞$ | {{field-5}} | $∞$ | $1$ | $0$ |"}] :knowledge-point/title "Building the Distance Matrix of a Graph"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105874 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105500 :question/id #uuid "1137706f-ac71-5049-9d26-8cee7f961492" :question/math-academy-id "e-16004" :question/problem "Consider the following graph.\n\n![](images/d0/d0dca731ce03691b5bf868071465b7ccb500f9c9e3fe824b43c9c1251c832bdf.png)\n\nFill in the missing values in the following distance matrix for the graph above.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ | $v_5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ |  |  | $3$ | $4$ |\n| $v_2$ | $2$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $v_3$ | $1$ | $2$ |  | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $v_4$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $v_5$ | $1$ |  | $3$ | $4$ | $0$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a fixed order of vertices $v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n$ in a directed graph, the *distance matrix* $D$ of the graph is an $n \\times n$-matrix (table), where the element $d_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column equals the distance from the vertex $v_i$ to the vertex $v_j$.\n\nIn our case, the vertices are arranged as $v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4$, and $v_5$. Let's determine the distances.\n\nRecall that the distance matrix of a directed graph is not symmetric in general. Some of the distances are calculated as follows:\n\n- The distance from $v_1$ to $v_2$ is $1$ since the shortest path from $v_1$ to $v_2$ (namely, $v_1 \\to v_2$) has a length of $1$. Hence, we have\n$d_{12} = 1$.\n- The distance from $v_1$ to $v_3$ is $2$ since the shortest path from $v_1$ to $v_3$ (namely, $v_1 \\to v_2 \\to v_3$) has a length of $2$. Hence, we have\n$d_{13} = 2$.\n\n- The entry $d_{33}$ is the distance from $v_3$ to itself, which is always $0$. Hence,\n$d_{33} = 0$.\n- The distance from $v_5$ to $v_2$ is $2$ since the shortest path from $v_5$ to $v_2$ (namely, $v_5 \\to v_1 \\to v_2$) has a length of $2$. Hence,\n$d_{52} = 2$.\n\nTherefore, the distance matrix is as follows:\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ | $v_5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $0$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| $v_2$ | $2$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $v_3$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $v_4$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $v_5$ | $1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$ | $3$ | $4$ | $0$ |"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ebcfd62-99c4-5fd5-8ebb-94e2e0c71bd3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106225 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "8731403c-f651-5479-9765-8a99755d982b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103408} {:answer-field/id #uuid "a24c55ed-4043-523c-8665-d2918b3fcd0c" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103409} {:answer-field/id #uuid "a5954c93-dc19-5444-8f01-52b6c688d175" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103410} {:answer-field/id #uuid "3ba06b0c-ce64-55cc-8b91-9cede42a10ea" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103411} {:answer-field/id #uuid "4ac75a60-78d5-50d6-8d43-0c3e11f4d33b" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103412}] :question/id #uuid "4fd1c0f7-2d82-5eed-ba93-9f31b6ecf70b" :question/math-academy-id "q-243231" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cf78c372557bb35a41c1a071b1f8d7bf39806ce3400b0e61102e3f582a32483f.png)\n\nFill in the missing values in the distance matrix of the directed graph above:\n\n|  | $v_{1}$ | $v_{2}$ | $v_{3}$ | $v_{4}$ | $v_{5}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_{1}$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} | $1$ | {{field-2}} | $3$ |\n| $v_{2}$ | $∞$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $v_{3}$ | $∞$ | $∞$ | {{field-4}} | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $v_{4}$ | $∞$ | $∞$ | $∞$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $v_{5}$ | $∞$ | {{field-5}} | $∞$ | $∞$ | $0$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "fd49d22a-5a5f-5fc6-8904-15383209605c" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103682} {:answer-field/id #uuid "07fdab03-a013-568c-992c-77da72abd20a" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103683} {:answer-field/id #uuid "0f2ea53a-fd6d-5f4a-893e-ec0a5c247037" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103684} {:answer-field/id #uuid "5237a3b8-43ff-58f2-9749-f866e81d5d1f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103685} {:answer-field/id #uuid "797bb702-5f87-58fc-a15b-3d413dff5932" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103686}] :question/id #uuid "9bebcb07-0278-5376-8764-24825591bde1" :question/math-academy-id "q-258175" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d858a5f4d3ef2ab53427f1b048302ebb16ed350c742f3071f1df5ff67ffa12e0.png)\n\nFill in the missing values in the distance matrix of the directed graph above:\n\n|  | $v_{1}$ | $v_{2}$ | $v_{3}$ | $v_{4}$ | $v_{5}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_{1}$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} | $2$ | {{field-2}} | $4$ |\n| $v_{2}$ | $2$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $v_{3}$ | $1$ | $2$ | {{field-4}} | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $v_{4}$ | $4$ | $2$ | $3$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $v_{5}$ | $3$ | {{field-5}} | $2$ | $3$ | $0$ |"}] :knowledge-point/title "Building the Distance Matrix of a Directed Graph"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105875 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105501 :question/id #uuid "6af3c276-c2dc-5e4b-8d32-10ef2bef0238" :question/math-academy-id "e-16005" :question/problem "Consider the following directed weighted graph.\n\n![](images/53/533d34061a801dab8ddc970cdd2f90ab6f7335678b03af29a5d53bb63bffaaa6.png)\n\nFill in the missing values in the following distance matrix for the graph above.\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ | $v_5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ |  | $3$ |  | $6$ | $11$ |\n| $v_2$ | $\\infty$ | $0$ | $4$ | $3$ |  |\n| $v_3$ | $\\infty$ |  | $0$ | $6$ | $11$ |\n| $v_4$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $0$ | $5$ |\n| $v_5$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $0$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a fixed order of vertices $v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n$ in a directed weighted graph, the *distance matrix* $D$ of the graph is an $n \\times n$-matrix (table), where the element $d_{ij}$ at the intersection of the $i$th row and the $j$th column equals the distance between the vertices $v_i$ and $v_j$.\n\nIf there is no path from $v_i$ to $v_j$, we define\n\n$$\nd_{ij}=\\infty\n$$\n\nIn our case, the vertices are arranged as $v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4$, and $v_5$. Some of the distance calculations are here as follows:\n\n- The entry $d_{11}$ is the distance from $v_1$ to itself, which is always zero. Hence,\n$d_{11} = 0$.\n- The distance from $v_1$ to $v_3$ is $7$ because the shortest path between these vertices (namely, $v_1 \\to v_2 \\to v_3$, not $v_1 \\to v_3$) has a total length of $3+4=7$. Hence, we have\n$d_{13} = 7$.\n- The distance from $v_2$ to $v_5$ is $8$ because the shortest path between these vertices (namely, $v_2 \\to v_4 \\to v_5$) has a total length of $3+5=8$. Hence, we have\n$d_{25} = 8$.\n- The distance from $v_3$ to $v_2$ is $\\infty$ because there is no path from $v_3$ to $v_3$. Hence, we have\n$d_{32} = \\infty$.\n\nTherefore, the distance matrix is as follows:\n\n|  | $v_1$ | $v_2$ | $v_3$ | $v_4$ | $v_5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_1$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$ | $3$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}7}$ | $6$ | $11$ |\n| $v_2$ | $\\infty$ | $0$ | $4$ | $3$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}8}$ |\n| $v_3$ | $\\infty$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\infty}$ | $0$ | $6$ | $11$ |\n| $v_4$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $0$ | $5$ |\n| $v_5$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $\\infty$ | $0$ |"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e142426-f58b-58c2-9807-aba3e0aeb40a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106226 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "93119438-032e-5f27-9830-b7e0218129b0" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103413} {:answer-field/id #uuid "d4404b05-4fde-53cc-93af-4d97b672ed81" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103414} {:answer-field/id #uuid "1b0e7fc2-42c3-5863-a088-d66d7e19e348" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103415} {:answer-field/id #uuid "54cf0fc9-f7f8-5d50-8a4c-6145458414fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103416} {:answer-field/id #uuid "5afe3a43-11aa-510d-b5f9-8ca2dae1dd5d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103417}] :question/id #uuid "eef770ba-4206-5cf0-878f-d264fda72bb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-243268" :question/problem "![](images/97/975e4efb738d0cc4de3d719544d4e8b6661e04579aff044293df262c76d07bd3.png)\n\nFill in the missing values in the distance matrix of the weighed graph above:\n\n|  | $v_{1}$ | $v_{2}$ | $v_{3}$ | $v_{4}$ | $v_{5}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_{1}$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} | $3$ | {{field-2}} | $11$ |\n| $v_{2}$ | $2$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $v_{3}$ | $3$ | $1$ | {{field-4}} | $3$ | $8$ |\n| $v_{4}$ | $6$ | $4$ | $3$ | $0$ | $5$ |\n| $v_{5}$ | $11$ | {{field-5}} | $8$ | $5$ | $0$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "8bd79818-d6b7-5a59-8dce-717d652d811f" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103418} {:answer-field/id #uuid "e0ec9809-112e-5ebd-a5bf-e2eaa0854dea" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103419} {:answer-field/id #uuid "00423657-067c-59a8-b798-813ceaaf2a2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103420} {:answer-field/id #uuid "2aebfe5a-375c-5aa0-9e05-b9e838072150" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103421} {:answer-field/id #uuid "44d28214-5891-5dc3-8e51-b9409b70f0c2" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186103422}] :question/id #uuid "3bbd4862-c8e6-5649-bee1-662f4647364d" :question/math-academy-id "q-243269" :question/problem "![](images/64/648d2b00bddc42411d0718e8185405230e2d8c51ecf48d60b39e2b4b3a52dd4c.png)\n\nFill in the missing values in the distance matrix of the directed weighted graph above:\n\n|  | $v_{1}$ | $v_{2}$ | $v_{3}$ | $v_{4}$ | $v_{5}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $v_{1}$ | $0$ | {{field-1}} | $3$ | {{field-2}} | $11$ |\n| $v_{2}$ | $∞$ | $0$ | $1$ | $4$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $v_{3}$ | $∞$ | $∞$ | {{field-4}} | $3$ | $8$ |\n| $v_{4}$ | $∞$ | $∞$ | $∞$ | $0$ | $5$ |\n| $v_{5}$ | $∞$ | {{field-5}} | $∞$ | $∞$ | $0$ |"}] :knowledge-point/title "Building the Distance Matrix of a Weighted Graph"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1392 :topic/title "Distance Matrices"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205074} :activity/id #uuid "dec8292b-04ba-57b4-8720-afabf5599b8a" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205074 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205206 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we learned that the principal components of a standardized data matrix $X$ are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix, and that these eigenvectors appear as the columns of $V$ in the singular value decomposition $X = U\\Sigma V^T.$ We also learned that the variance along each principal component is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to use principal components to perform **feature extraction** — the process of projecting high-dimensional data onto a lower-dimensional subspace while preserving as much variance as possible.\n\nTo illustrate the idea, suppose a biologist has recorded the resting heart rate and breathing rate for four mammals of different sizes. Both measurements have been standardized, giving the $4 \\times 2$ data matrix\n\n![](images/c6/c6952e27be521fed69a3e9ff76520b01873fdc3e3e28f0015550cbdc8cda3110.svg)\n\nThe biologist knows that body size strongly influences both heart rate and breathing rate — smaller animals tend to have faster rates for both. They want to extract a single \"metabolic index\" that captures this underlying factor. How should they proceed?\n\nFrom the SVD $X = U\\Sigma V^T,$ the first principal component is\n\n![](images/82/82a177ec2b7448d62d171a249e464d5da15308e41a6140b156d2d50d2c251f00.svg)\n\nwhich points in the direction of maximum variance. To obtain a one-dimensional representation of the data, we project each row of $X$ onto this direction by computing\n\n![](images/39/39fa24e5c2dbec4ceb7a56c0ee17f9c4996d08a2d9e1bc91d0849d7e24abe5c1.svg)\n\nThe result is a $4 \\times 1$ vector — one number per animal — that captures the variation in body size. The second animal, with the most negative standardized values (indicating a larger, slower-metabolizing animal), has the largest projected value." :tutorial/id #uuid "894cce51-8da3-55a0-8188-37b91ca5c47e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13253 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "95bf8e11-91d4-5faa-b4b9-f34fccb4a50e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205075}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205075 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205213 :tutorial/content "More generally, suppose we have a standardized $n \\times p$ data matrix $X,$ where each row is a data point and each column is a feature. To reduce the data from $p$ dimensions to $k$ dimensions, we project the data onto the first $k$ principal components. If $V_k$ is the $p \\times k$ matrix whose columns are the first $k$ principal components, the reduced data matrix is\n\n$$\nZ = X V_k.\n$$\n\nEach row of $Z$ is a $k$-dimensional representation of the corresponding data point, expressed in terms of the principal component directions.\n\nThere is an equivalent way to compute $Z$ using the SVD. Starting from $X = U\\Sigma V^T,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & =X{V}_{k} \\\\  & =UΣ{V}^{T}{V}_{k} \\\\  & =UΣ[\\begin{aligned}{I}_{k} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U[\\begin{aligned}{Σ}_{k} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={U}_{k}{Σ}_{k},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $U_k$ is the $n \\times k$ matrix of the first $k$ columns of $U,$ and $\\Sigma_k$ is the $k \\times k$ diagonal matrix of the first $k$ singular values. \n\nSo, we can compute the reduced data either as $XV_k$ or as $U_k\\Sigma_k.$\n\n**Watch out!** The vector $v_1$ (the first column of $V$) is a *direction* — a unit vector in feature space pointing along maximum variance. By contrast, the product $Xv_1$ (equivalently, $u_1\\sigma_1$) is the *projected data* — an $n \\times 1$ vector giving each data point's coordinate along that direction. Some texts refer to this projected data as \"the first principal component of the data\" or as \"scores,\" so the term \"principal component\" can mean either the direction or the projected values, depending on context." :tutorial/id #uuid "41d5d02d-cb8d-54bf-9edc-b6d69f78b42e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14748 :tutorial/title "The Projection Formula"} :step/id #uuid "7cac9d17-5846-58de-95e6-7e0f8e56e0ac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205076}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205076 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de17e8f9-e3dc-5ba0-b6c9-327aa8cc10e0"} :step/id #uuid "74cf39f4-5e68-568e-bccb-a900bb795de7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205077}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205214 :tutorial/content "In the previous section, we saw how to project data onto the first $k$ principal components. But how do we choose $k?$ Keeping more components preserves more information, while keeping fewer gives a more compact representation at the cost of discarding some variation.\n\nRecall that the variance along the $i$th principal component is proportional to $\\sigma_i^2,$ where $\\sigma_i$ is the $i$th singular value. Therefore, the fraction of total variance preserved by keeping the first $k$ components is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sigma_1^2 + \\sigma_2^2 + \\cdots + \\sigma_k^2}{\\sigma_1^2 + \\sigma_2^2 + \\cdots + \\sigma_p^2} = \\dfrac{\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\sigma_i^2}{\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{p} \\sigma_i^2}.\n$$\n\nWhile the amount of variance we need to preserve depends on the application, as a rule of thumb, we will choose the smallest $k$ that preserves at least $90\\%$ of the variance. That is, we select $k$ such that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\sigma_i^2}{\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{p} \\sigma_i^2} \\geq 0.90.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "97dbcb11-96a6-5a5f-aafc-7d212236c53c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14749 :tutorial/title "Choosing the Number of Components"} :step/id #uuid "0f3228b2-720c-5166-8dbe-667a26a03e21" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bbf7902-3a15-5dd0-b2c8-e5b7fd541345"} :step/id #uuid "e5823a48-e0f6-565c-9756-9e43c2763e07" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205215 :tutorial/content "We can now summarize the complete pipeline for using PCA to extract features from a dataset.\n\n1\\. **Standardize the data.** Subtract the mean of each feature and divide by its standard deviation. This ensures that all features contribute equally to the analysis, regardless of their original units or scales.\n\n2\\. **Compute the SVD.** Find the singular value decomposition $X_{\\text{std}} = U\\Sigma V^T.$ The columns of $V$ are the principal components, and the diagonal entries of $\\Sigma$ are the singular values.\n\n3\\. **Choose the number of components.** Using the singular values, compute the cumulative variance for each possible choice of $k$ and select the smallest $k$ that preserves at least $90\\%$ of the variance.\n\n4\\. **Project the data.** Compute the reduced data matrix $Z = X_{\\text{std}} V_k,$ where $V_k$ consists of the first $k$ columns of $V.$\n\nTo interpret results in terms of the original features or to apply the same transformation to new data, we must keep track of the means and standard deviations used in Step 1." :tutorial/id #uuid "3cf2cdec-09e3-5344-84d2-4617e6830871" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14750 :tutorial/title "The Feature Extraction Pipeline"} :step/id #uuid "cc9242f2-052f-51db-9396-bda401bd5870" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205080}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204107 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45f11c96-89e5-5662-81b6-06cff80a4cf2"} :step/id #uuid "f79c44a0-cf86-5bde-a18b-20df7fdcba79"}] :activity/title "Feature Extraction Using PCA" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186204154} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543632} :activity/id #uuid "82585ae6-b97e-5a0f-b4ec-6a2fa9b638ea" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543599 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543563 :question/id #uuid "d96fd40c-87e8-5ffa-bd33-b4b724c33445"} :step/id #uuid "0f025f10-87f0-5230-aa1e-c4fc72c4b0be"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204604 :question/id #uuid "3805f753-7550-5fde-b9cf-db9f171fd376"} :step/id #uuid "553d33d3-b10f-56a9-99e1-f576e4037e27" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543660}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543640 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543515 :question/id #uuid "6d89bf53-876c-5233-a22b-c8fa7b16fa9b"} :step/id #uuid "67885360-0e56-54e7-b8aa-2c07475cf1c1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543599}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543481 :question/id #uuid "1c7b66a2-e4ef-5d7b-9466-353945dad93a"} :step/id #uuid "be5846ee-0350-5ca1-9749-42cc259d4241" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543640}}] :activity/title "Feature Extraction Using PCA" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542623}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186050839 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204105 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203875 :question/id #uuid "d6f2c4b7-eb2d-5d32-bcd8-8ec9443c14da" :question/math-academy-id "e-18786" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4 \\times 2$ matrix\n![](images/23/232f6bffd41437d85b7a0f09ec268d459009656ef77fba4b4fb445b52dd13a1d.svg)\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X = U \\Sigma V^T$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.689 & -0.424 & 0.495 & 0.318 \\\\ 0.143 & -0.241 & -0.524 & 0.804 \\\\ 0.695 & -0.188 & 0.650 & 0.244 \\\\ -0.149 & 0.853 & 0.241 & 0.439\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.075 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.294 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.709 & -0.706 \\\\ 0.706 & -0.709\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance." :question/worked-solution "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $X^TX.$ Using the SVD $X = U\\Sigma V^T$ and the orthogonality of $U,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\Sigma^T\\Sigma$ is diagonal with the squared singular values on the diagonal, the eigenvectors of $X^TX$ are the columns of $V,$ and the eigenvalues are the squared singular values. The largest eigenvalue corresponds to the first singular value $\\sigma_1 = 2.075,$ so the first principal component is $v_1,$ the first column of $V{:}$\n\n![](images/e9/e91c75818de82c4564584df51a72432ebc97c90dd5a1c8687b24bd31998d9910.svg)\n\nTo obtain the one-dimensional version of the data, we project $X$ onto $v_1$ by computing\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X{v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1.40 & -0.62 \\\\ 0.01 & 0.43 \\\\ -0.85 & 1.19 \\\\ -0.56 & -1.00\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-0.709 \\\\ 0.706\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1.43 \\\\ 0.30 \\\\ 1.44 \\\\ -0.31\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, we can compute this projection using $u_1 \\sigma_1,$ where $u_1$ is the first column of $U.$ Indeed, from the SVD,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X{v}_{1} & =UΣ{V}^{T}{v}_{1}=UΣ[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=U[\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={u}_{1}{\\sigma }_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u_1$ and $\\sigma_1 = 2.075,$ we get\n\n![](images/14/148bc9bd4289245d9ada00da68f7b6e0666bfa9c3fffd47fde79164b177732d8.svg)\n\nwhich confirms the result.\n\n**Watch out!** The vector $v_1$ itself is the first principal component *direction* — a unit vector in feature space. It is not a one-dimensional representation of the data. The one-dimensional representation is $Xv_1$ (or equivalently $u_1\\sigma_1$), which is a $4 \\times 1$ vector containing the projection of each data point onto this direction."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de17e8f9-e3dc-5ba0-b6c9-327aa8cc10e0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204594 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c17b053-ee56-58a3-b5ab-480746b485c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203480} {:answer/id #uuid "976a48b1-98c8-5301-b839-507339612de3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203481} {:answer/id #uuid "b04ff4b7-d18b-5532-89f6-e898f679b206" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203482} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce54b1c-c9de-5be1-8fa2-77261f7a755d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203483} {:answer/id #uuid "5b1f08a5-6a87-5d5d-abde-83396a104946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203484}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f7fd4941-b366-5b8a-b793-67d8eceb04ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203479}] :question/id #uuid "e0791293-64c2-5bd1-a4d7-59acebba08a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-287059" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4 \\cdot 2$ matrix\n$X = \\begin{bmatrix}0.40 & 0.21 \\\\ -1.49 & -1.44 \\\\ 0.42 & 0.85 \\\\ 0.67 & 0.38\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X = UΣV^{T}$ where:\n\n$U|= \\begin{bmatrix}-0.179 & -0.341 & 0.574 & -0.723 \\\\ 0.858 & 0.08 & 0.489 & 0.138 \\\\ -0.371 & 0.78 & 0.491 & 0.114 \\\\ -0.307 & -0.519 & 0.437 & 0.667\\end{bmatrix}; Σ|= \\begin{bmatrix}2.416 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.392 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}; V|= \\begin{bmatrix}-0.708 & -0.706 \\\\ -0.706 & 0.708\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\n1. $\\begin{bmatrix}0.40 & 0.21 \\\\ -1.49 & -1.44 \\\\ 0.42 & 0.85 \\\\ 0.67 & 0.38\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-0.708 \\\\ -0.706\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $\\begin{bmatrix}-0.179 \\\\ 0.858 \\\\ -0.371 \\\\ -0.307\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot 2.416$\n3. $\\begin{bmatrix}-0.708 \\\\ -0.706\\end{bmatrix}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204611 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82f7cefd-63a9-5d5e-bafb-9f9f32cddd1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203556} {:answer/id #uuid "84693c83-a43b-544e-922a-8b5c42cff503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203557} {:answer/id #uuid "aff6adc6-f95b-5086-a9c7-25f8d354e438" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203558} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f22aa1-5d8c-5b88-902a-90419f8f4734" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203559} {:answer/id #uuid "c1654876-24a3-506f-b854-ac27880cf2e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203560}] :answer-field/id #uuid "902850bb-3636-5dad-8292-be9045c66232" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203555}] :question/id #uuid "b02a73e1-f9d8-5470-acb2-d5d8ec5ea8a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-355828" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4 \\cdot 2$ matrix\n$X = \\begin{bmatrix}1.2 & 0.02 \\\\ -0.27 & 1.05 \\\\ -1.19 & -1.35 \\\\ 0.27 & 0.27\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X = UΣV^{T}$ where:\n\n$U|= \\begin{bmatrix}-0.411 & 0.663 & 0.61 & -0.141 \\\\ -0.262 & -0.743 & 0.606 & -0.113 \\\\ 0.854 & 0.091 & 0.501 & 0.105 \\\\ -0.182 & 0 & 0.104 & 0.978\\end{bmatrix}; Σ|= \\begin{bmatrix}2.102 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.257 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}; V|= \\begin{bmatrix}-0.708 & 0.707 \\\\ -0.707 & -0.708\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\n1. $\\begin{bmatrix}-0.708 \\\\ -0.707\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $\\begin{bmatrix}-0.411 \\\\ 0.663 \\\\ 0.61 \\\\ -0.141\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot 2.102$\n3. $\\begin{bmatrix}-0.411 \\\\ -0.262 \\\\ 0.854 \\\\ -0.182\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot 2.102$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529096 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "158c5e57-6484-540e-8dae-1f773f268a50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528353} {:answer/id #uuid "36559b24-53e6-5745-8a45-9fd571a78363" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528438} {:answer/id #uuid "4c09282e-d0f3-5c75-9b06-861585a630c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528489} {:answer/id #uuid "968f6982-b7e0-5819-8694-ea0749ae1d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528655} {:answer/id #uuid "e36fb8c0-2d56-5128-88b0-bc68d95661d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528489} :answer-field/id #uuid "07619765-e1d4-5971-ad43-4b34eeca2055" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528941}] :question/id #uuid "17f34adc-72ed-5271-a021-3a7f1d773e6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-355834" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.98 & -0.67 \\\\ 0.52 & 1.49 \\\\ 1.14 & -0.36 \\\\ -0.69 & -0.46\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.56 & -0.17 & 0.71 & -0.393 \\\\ 0.682 & -0.53 & 0.486 & 0.135 \\\\ 0.264 & 0.821 & 0.485 & 0.144 \\\\ -0.39 & -0.126 & 0.159 & 0.898\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.085 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.292 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.707 & 0.708 \\\\ 0.708 & -0.707\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.708\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}-0.98 & -0.67\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.708\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}-0.98 & -0.67 \\\\ 0.52 & 1.49 \\\\ 1.14 & -0.36 \\\\ -0.69 & -0.46\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.708\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option II represents the projection of only the first datapoint (the first row of $X$) onto the first principal component. Therefore, it produces a single scalar value rather than the full one-dimensional dataset.\n\n- Option III corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Indeed, it represents $X\\cdot {v}_{1},$ which is exactly the projection of the full dataset $X$ onto the first principal component.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529104 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a907ca2-c523-5180-bcdc-bbe6f6c4642e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528372} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc0fdcf-4cb8-5018-a940-071de4027293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528645} {:answer/id #uuid "9d42bdf7-d6e9-55e1-86ce-2e629a5f8fc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528677} {:answer/id #uuid "ee184618-27b0-5c25-9140-55a9d8379731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528889} {:answer/id #uuid "f805cefc-a8ea-5ac2-a5c1-3ba59d32a17f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528889} :answer-field/id #uuid "87da0997-109c-5d59-8556-8db99822494e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529016}] :question/id #uuid "2633d33a-0706-5b9b-a985-6a0050cda4a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-355831" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.27 & 0.39 \\\\ -0.99 & -1.23 \\\\ -0.13 & -0.28 \\\\ 1.39 & 1.12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.035 & 0.86 & 0.132 & 0.491 \\\\ -0.657 & -0.314 & -0.208 & 0.653 \\\\ -0.121 & -0.196 & 0.969 & 0.092 \\\\ 0.743 & -0.351 & -0.032 & 0.569\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.389 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.542 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.707\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}0.035 \\\\ -0.657 \\\\ -0.121 \\\\ 0.743\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 2.389$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.035 \\\\ 0.86 \\\\ 0.132 \\\\ 0.491\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 2.389$" :question/worked-solution "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Notice that it represents ${u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1},$ where ${u}_{1}$ is the first column of $U$ and ${\\sigma }_{1}$ is the first singular value. But this equals  $X\\cdot {v}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cdot {v}_{1} & =UΣ{V}^{T}\\cdot {v}_{1}=UΣ\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=U\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Option II represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option III uses the values of the first row of $U$ multiplied by ${\\sigma }_{1}.$ However, the projection corresponds to the first column of $U.$ Thus, using the row vector is incorrect.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f38a1d5-5e26-5720-933a-074463f63291" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528462} {:answer/id #uuid "8eed707b-6371-5999-839b-4f5cd51042db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528642} {:answer/id #uuid "93bad0d2-4d8d-5501-8d17-ee62bd3e4da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528651} {:answer/id #uuid "9a843bc1-b154-5a46-a21d-415e36ea992a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528668} {:answer/id #uuid "d27dd8c5-0a47-555c-b549-d93700db5c64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528668} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0babfda-6373-531b-92eb-9560a146bc71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529028}] :question/id #uuid "3689d124-7069-5ac1-b495-7dcfa807134a" :question/math-academy-id "q-355838" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.33 & 1.18 \\\\ -0.49 & -0.88 \\\\ 1.49 & 0.48 \\\\ -0.66 & -0.77\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.29 & 0.813 & 0.505 & 0.021 \\\\ -0.469 & -0.212 & 0.634 & -0.578 \\\\ 0.676 & -0.54 & 0.473 & 0.168 \\\\ -0.49 & -0.061 & 0.345 & 0.798\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.065 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.316 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.709 & -0.705 \\\\ 0.705 & 0.709\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}0.709 \\\\ 0.705\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}-0.33 & 1.18 \\\\ -0.49 & -0.88 \\\\ 1.49 & 0.48 \\\\ -0.66 & -0.77\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.709 \\\\ 0.705\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.29 \\\\ -0.469 \\\\ 0.676 \\\\ -0.49\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 2.065$" :question/worked-solution "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option II corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Indeed, it represents $X\\cdot {v}_{1},$ which is exactly the projection of $X$ onto the first principal component.\n\n- Option III corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Notice that it represents ${u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1},$ where ${u}_{1}$ is the first column of $U$ and ${\\sigma }_{1}$ is the first singular value. But this equals  $X\\cdot {v}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cdot {v}_{1} & =UΣ{V}^{T}\\cdot {v}_{1}=UΣ\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=U\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124dd69d-b9ac-532a-85d8-fd7b70eecf8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528342} {:answer/id #uuid "36ca5ef3-24a6-5fb1-8622-0b1d7a9443b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528441} {:answer/id #uuid "3ebbbe6b-7c84-5932-a277-a4405916dccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528460} {:answer/id #uuid "bddffe44-aacb-5f0d-a38d-a2f7fc732660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528764} {:answer/id #uuid "ff685870-d8f7-56d3-a9cf-f7dffe500056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528936} :answer-field/id #uuid "0408fb21-e6d7-5afd-8fc7-4e0712c6e323" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528940}] :question/id #uuid "5bab4cd0-ae02-5029-8d61-df09bcee19e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-355832" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0.866 & 1.212 \\\\ 0.866 & -0.173 \\\\ -0.866 & -1.212 \\\\ -0.866 & 0.173\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.670 & 0.224 & 0.224 & -0.671 \\\\ 0.224 & -0.671 & 0.671 & 0.224 \\\\ -0.670 & -0.224 & 0.224 & -0.671 \\\\ -0.224 & 0.671 & 0.671 & 0.224\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.191 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.095 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.707\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}0.866 & 1.212\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.866 & 1.212 \\\\ 0.866 & -0.173 \\\\ -0.866 & -1.212 \\\\ -0.866 & 0.173\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I represents the projection of only the first datapoint (the first row of $X$) onto the first principal component. Therefore, it produces a single scalar value rather than the full one-dimensional dataset.\n\n- Option II corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Indeed, it represents $X\\cdot {v}_{1},$ which is exactly the projection of the full dataset $X$ onto the first principal component.\n\n- Option III represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "117e5dbc-80f8-532a-8d97-3cd9d790be5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528340} {:answer/id #uuid "14ba44ce-54ad-50d5-b6be-6a4ff5b285b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528351} {:answer/id #uuid "a348c40f-1571-51e0-9281-80844369699e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528695} {:answer/id #uuid "a77b766c-7b5b-551f-8c98-f81dfcdda2c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528705} {:answer/id #uuid "d27bd917-8a17-55cb-9e24-d1df3af18407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528351} :answer-field/id #uuid "48e3fb47-9a79-5df7-a139-217361ff8523" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528983}] :question/id #uuid "6b5fc782-a821-5ef2-823c-11a0d288b2b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-355836" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-1.26 & 0.32 \\\\ 0.06 & -1.27 \\\\ 1.19 & -0.18 \\\\ 0.02 & 1.12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.583 & -0.434 & 0.687 & 0.016 \\\\ 0.49 & -0.561 & 0.046 & 0.666 \\\\ 0.506 & 0.466 & 0.725 & -0.029 \\\\ -0.405 & 0.529 & -0.027 & 0.746\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}1.917 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.528 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.709 & 0.706 \\\\ -0.706 & 0.709\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $[\\begin{aligned}-0.583 \\\\ 0.49 \\\\ 0.506 \\\\ -0.405\\end{aligned}]\\cdot 1.917$\n\nII. $[\\begin{aligned}0.709 \\\\ -0.706\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $[\\begin{aligned}-1.26 & 0.32 \\\\ 0.06 & -1.27 \\\\ 1.19 & -0.18 \\\\ 0.02 & 1.12\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0.709 \\\\ -0.706\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "The one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance is the projection onto the first principal component.\n\nRecall that the principal components are the eigenvectors of the covariance matrix ${X}^{T}X.$\n\nUsing the SVD and the fact that $U$ is orthogonal (i.e., ${U}^{T}U=I$), we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{T}X & =(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}{U}^{T}UΣ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V{Σ}^{T}Σ{V}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $Σ$ is a diagonal matrix, and so is ${Σ}^{T}Σ,$ we know that the eigenvectors of ${X}^{T}X$ are the columns of $V.$\n\nIn particular, the first principal component is the eigenvector of ${X}^{T}X$ corresponding to the largest eigenvalue. \nAnd since $Σ$ contains the corresponding singular values (square roots of the eigenvalues) in decreasing order, the first principal component is ${v}_{1},$ the first column of $V.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Option I corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Notice that it represents ${u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1},$ where ${u}_{1}$ is the first column of $U$ and ${\\sigma }_{1}$ is the first singular value. But this equals $X\\cdot {v}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cdot {v}_{1} & =UΣ{V}^{T}\\cdot {v}_{1}=UΣ\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=U\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={u}_{1}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Option II represents ${v}_{1},$ which is the first principal component. It represents a direction in feature space, not a version of the data. Notice that it is also dimensionally incorrect, since it is a $2\\times 1$ matrix instead of $4\\times 1.$\n\n- Option III corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance. Indeed, it represents $X\\cdot {v}_{1},$ which is exactly the projection of $X$ onto the first principal component.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Best One Dimensional Reduction Given SVD of Data"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204106 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203876 :question/id #uuid "a17de4fa-f7c3-5038-b9fa-03780393b360" :question/math-academy-id "e-18840" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5 \\times 3$ matrix\n![](images/83/83365da559075cf32eaebf67a12eef548f40585a84a3226aad488327de8e9e84.svg)\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X = U \\Sigma V^T$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.75 & 0.177 & 0.014 & 0.637 & -0.004 \\\\ 0.275 & -0.054 & -0.847 & 0.36 & 0.274 \\\\ -0.213 & -0.653 & 0.171 & -0.068 & 0.703 \\\\ 0.542 & -0.181 & 0.459 & 0.678 & -0.05 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.711 & 0.206 & -0.022 & 0.655\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.272 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.031 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.659 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.724 & 0.019 & 0.69 \\\\ 0.422 & 0.779 & -0.464 \\\\ 0.546 & -0.627 & -0.555\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nFill in the blanks below:\n\nWe should pick components based on \n![](images/21/21182b5f0667421011af540ed046b0924b738d74d6a09f574320bd4768df7c38.svg)\n\nIf we wish to preserve $75\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n![](images/ab/abbb30d852a431e139075cbe6686ea1aafd697e5d52351e1f23730545ea2d661.svg)." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n\\sigma_1 = 2.272, \\qquad\n\\sigma_2 = 2.031, \\qquad\n\\sigma_3 = 1.659.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sigma_1^2}{\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^3 \\sigma_i^2} =\n\\dfrac{2.272^2}{2.272^2 + 2.031^2 + 1.659^2} \\approx 42.9\\%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^2 \\sigma_i^2}{\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^3 \\sigma_i^2} =\n\\dfrac{2.272^2 + 2.031^2}{2.272^2 + 2.031^2 + 1.659^2} \\approx 77.1\\%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $42.9\\%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $77.1\\%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/f3/f35a54aa598a0c65fc14ae807b60ddd6dcb68a36dd4f0ba5089fae12ec7a9495.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to ![](images/8d/8d0e762e494aa7a29af18cae486e8fca07aa9e6fb1c68e1204b8e1765caee8c2.svg) since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $75\\%$ of the variance."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bbf7902-3a15-5dd0-b2c8-e5b7fd541345" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204595 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0c0003e-f20a-5f03-89e7-29bd9654c014" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203486} {:answer/id #uuid "08a2f97d-f6de-5269-b0fa-cd339da42efe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203487} {:answer/id #uuid "327c6e12-7c7e-5837-b030-051257838bd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203488} {:answer/id #uuid "7b859f99-5562-5516-bf56-c34c349e3ea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203489}] :answer-field/id #uuid "6d6e308f-920b-5874-83fa-6f49096d0d1f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203485}] :question/id #uuid "b174d44b-27ae-5ebe-bcb9-b02442f4945a" :question/math-academy-id "q-287648" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5 \\cdot 3$ matrix\n$X = \\begin{bmatrix}1.40 & 1.37 & -0.49 \\\\ 0.59 & 0.29 & -0.70 \\\\ -0.97 & -1.18 & -0.66 \\\\ -0.15 & 0.27 & 1.67 \\\\ -0.86 & -0.75 & 0.18\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X = UΣV^{T}$ where:\n\n$U|= \\begin{bmatrix}-0.713 & 0.123 & -0.47 & 0.506 & 0.013 \\\\ -0.25 & 0.321 & 0.709 & 0.213 & 0.535 \\\\ 0.509 & 0.422 & 0.046 & 0.667 & -0.342 \\\\ 0.042 & -0.839 & 0.241 & 0.487 & 0.01 \\\\ 0.41 & -0.027 & -0.465 & 0.133 & 0.773\\end{bmatrix}; Σ|= \\begin{bmatrix}2.809 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.020 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.114 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}; V|= \\begin{bmatrix}-0.711 & 0.051 & 0.701 \\\\ -0.693 & -0.218 & -0.687 \\\\ 0.118 & -0.975 & 0.191\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset.\n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on\n{{field-1}}."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204612 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6f25b04-4b91-598e-a565-be3663d6a848" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203562} {:answer/id #uuid "c72dffd8-1d94-5753-ab91-0de31a1bf0dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203563} {:answer/id #uuid "5c4134ab-9707-5b12-b3b2-b92ef0ef55b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203564} {:answer/id #uuid "7a1c7755-a2c7-544b-8147-4c1e1d7335fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203562} :answer-field/id #uuid "673f2cfc-466c-5530-bf38-03cc0d0b363a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203561} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e228fd-1f0b-50d7-9082-a4911c109df9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528404} {:answer/id #uuid "38b85c46-47af-5d2f-a564-e9efc73d7eec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528444} {:answer/id #uuid "63ef7549-c7c2-58ca-92bb-1912806cb967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528536} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd67190-901e-54cf-963f-8fa2f4f87575" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528800} :answer-field/id #uuid "0935b382-4574-59fb-a9b6-ea0fdb98be37" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528942}] :question/id #uuid "d3d0f846-dcec-5a1e-a313-ed34c8131231" :question/math-academy-id "q-355899" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5\\times 4$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-1.11 & -1.51 & -0.99 & -1.19 \\\\ -0.58 & 1.16 & 1.49 & 0.35 \\\\ -0.29 & 0.03 & 0.04 & -0.68 \\\\ 1.43 & 0.57 & -0.84 & 1.42 \\\\ 0.55 & -0.25 & 0.3 & 0.1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.761 & 0.185 & 0.263 & 0.342 & 0.447 \\\\ 0.309 & -0.738 & 0.28 & 0.285 & 0.447 \\\\ -0.169 & -0.143 & -0.022 & -0.866 & 0.447 \\\\ 0.538 & 0.63 & 0.338 & 0.013 & 0.447 \\\\ 0.084 & 0.067 & -0.859 & 0.225 & 0.447\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}3.125 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.362 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.699 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0.455 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.49 & 0.509 & -0.625 & -0.333 \\\\ 0.572 & -0.337 & 0.479 & -0.573 \\\\ 0.25 & -0.761 & -0.55 & 0.237 \\\\ 0.608 & 0.22 & 0.278 & 0.71\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=3.125,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=2.362,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=0.699,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.455.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.125}^{2}}{{3.125}^{2}+{2.362}^{2}+{0.699}^{2}+{0.455}^{2}}≈60.9%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.125}^{2}+{2.362}^{2}}{{3.125}^{2}+{2.362}^{2}+{0.699}^{2}+{0.455}^{2}}≈95.7%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $60.9%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $95.7%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/29/299ec97726335bb0dc02c7d256c7722eb52a7c22cd8b46a856d1b669bbe918dc.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90%$ of the variance."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1aa5625e-af4e-5cb0-be77-41320df40a0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528373} {:answer/id #uuid "29775c65-a127-562d-905b-ce5a0a1506c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528403} {:answer/id #uuid "bd240f70-4ee4-553a-b8a1-f11f13074026" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528760} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c6296bc-3942-5538-b3c5-b580c49e804f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528946} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3fa802e-1a7c-5131-9274-0f3fd251222c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528697} {:answer/id #uuid "abe5576c-3f57-582e-a2f6-b681ef2c6ece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528717} {:answer/id #uuid "ba0f7340-726e-57e2-8594-d7697b1e2ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528754} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa5d643-96f3-5f51-b2d9-8f6ea8ab456c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528754} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dfae55b-dcf9-5c8f-99dd-37e10549a26b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529027}] :question/id #uuid "3158298f-c612-536d-8b25-00f57fa2f77e" :question/math-academy-id "q-355844" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0.74 & 0.61 & 0.89 \\\\ 0.38 & 0 & 0.61 \\\\ -0.92 & -0.03 & -0.87 \\\\ -1.2 & -1.61 & -1.28 \\\\ 1.01 & 1.02 & 0.65\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.389 & 0.167 & -0.312 & 0.84 & -0.133 \\\\ -0.174 & 0.453 & -0.384 & -0.192 & 0.762 \\\\ 0.321 & -0.749 & -0.348 & 0.232 & 0.402 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.384 & 0.359 & 0.419 & 0.22 \\\\ -0.464 & -0.242 & 0.712 & 0.166 & 0.438\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}3.329 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.311 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.591 & 0.31 & 0.745 \\\\ -0.558 & -0.823 & -0.1 \\\\ -0.582 & 0.475 & -0.66\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $95%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=3.329,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=0.9,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=0.311.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.329}^{2}}{{3.329}^{2}+{0.9}^{2}+{0.311}^{2}}≈92.4%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.329}^{2}+{0.9}^{2}}{{3.329}^{2}+{0.9}^{2}+{0.311}^{2}}≈99.2%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $92.4%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $99.2%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/e2/e2af29d0509f7ae3e3826cd35d3190b478e9c2415c00fc0e3798800d33381c59.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $95%$ of the variance."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e00e59c-c2e1-5d95-88f6-75a0287bc55a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528417} {:answer/id #uuid "359ded72-a1d6-5847-8db5-fe5d7574386d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528437} {:answer/id #uuid "64e8fa21-4152-565d-bf5a-4add5225dbab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528540} {:answer/id #uuid "8dd94c47-0c6d-5b9d-a0a0-01f30dc5aa0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528540} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c994f1c-25ce-53c1-b2bc-ad51547e017a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528999} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b9077d-bc80-505b-8b3d-48730306347d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528347} {:answer/id #uuid "162330ce-ca49-5e79-89b6-d89a13587017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528355} {:answer/id #uuid "beb61f16-161b-5af3-980b-30635c4dc9c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528765} {:answer/id #uuid "c1060128-a39b-5154-8642-ecf7080795dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528772} :answer-field/id #uuid "8575b8f8-052d-504c-a3e1-9051091e1d1f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529014}] :question/id #uuid "439142f8-6932-5f54-8d84-ca7931d891df" :question/math-academy-id "q-355908" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5\\times 4$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.36 & 1.7 & 0.25 & 1.05 \\\\ 0.8 & -0.33 & -1.37 & -0.53 \\\\ -0.51 & -0.23 & 0.05 & -0.61 \\\\ -1.18 & -0.96 & -0.32 & -1.01 \\\\ 1.25 & -0.18 & 1.4 & 1.1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}0.477 & 0.704 & -0.239 & 0.135 & 0.448 \\\\ -0.291 & -0.357 & -0.764 & 0.049 & 0.449 \\\\ -0.217 & 0.106 & 0.24 & -0.83 & 0.442 \\\\ -0.55 & 0.135 & 0.445 & 0.529 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.582 & -0.589 & 0.323 & 0.104 & 0.447\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}3.041 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.967 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.671 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0.268 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.356 & -0.757 & -0.46 & -0.298 \\\\ 0.454 & 0.644 & -0.416 & -0.454 \\\\ 0.493 & -0.1 & 0.783 & -0.366 \\\\ 0.652 & 0.041 & -0.052 & 0.755\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=3.041,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=1.967,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1.671,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.268.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.041}^{2}}{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}+{0.268}^{2}}≈57.9%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}}{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}+{0.268}^{2}}≈82.1%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first three principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}}{{3.041}^{2}+{1.967}^{2}+{1.671}^{2}+{0.268}^{2}}≈99.6%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $57.9%$ of the variance,\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $82.1%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $3$ dimensions would preserve $99.6%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/5e/5eb43d1adcfa48f0fd66cd73ea05b40a9e937399457ba8614f380502c1c632dc.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $3\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90%$ of the variance."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529155 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fc905bf-a333-5f88-bc94-7532cf83b587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528419} {:answer/id #uuid "7d6ed0a8-2992-50b5-a253-5ba14a0a4402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528597} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe3ce25-405b-53e4-8190-780d13dc0791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528683} {:answer/id #uuid "c7676032-6f77-5131-a597-0e7248c6766a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528785} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf9d5ef7-04cb-5f23-a601-17a6256765aa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529040} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "270f63c7-1aeb-558c-8a65-03c3f71ad498" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528400} {:answer/id #uuid "3678144c-c5c5-59eb-9c73-7f7cfc99f4a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528440} {:answer/id #uuid "8334f0e6-38a4-5448-b6ae-13c404a2e8fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528613} {:answer/id #uuid "bb438be0-e790-540f-8d81-6dbdb5c89782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528613} :answer-field/id #uuid "d87761c7-3333-5f96-b1b1-7878fa8dd615" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529063}] :question/id #uuid "9ccff77b-7d01-5bd7-aaf4-9fba844066ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-355903" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5\\times 4$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0.46 & -0.2 & 0.07 & -0.88 \\\\ -1.4 & 0.15 & 0.73 & -0.87 \\\\ -0.24 & 1.53 & 0.68 & 1.35 \\\\ -0.15 & -1.25 & 0.23 & 0.73 \\\\ 1.32 & -0.23 & -1.72 & -0.32\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.186 & 0.257 & 0.252 & -0.792 & -0.456 \\\\ 0.398 & 0.593 & 0.3 & 0.45 & -0.443 \\\\ 0.529 & -0.708 & 0.123 & -0.057 & -0.447 \\\\ -0.015 & 0.115 & -0.887 & 0.015 & -0.446 \\\\ -0.725 & -0.259 & 0.208 & 0.408 & -0.443\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.878 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2.152 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.636 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0.652 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & -0.419 & 0.045 & -0.68 \\\\ 0.38 & -0.525 & 0.761 & 0.04 \\\\ 0.654 & 0.205 & -0.148 & -0.713 \\\\ 0.262 & -0.712 & -0.63 & 0.166\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=2.878,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=2.152,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1.636,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.652.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.878}^{2}}{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}+{1.636}^{2}+{0.652}^{2}}≈51.7%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}}{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}+{1.636}^{2}+{0.652}^{2}}≈80.6%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first three principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}+{1.636}^{2}}{{2.878}^{2}+{2.152}^{2}+{1.636}^{2}+{0.652}^{2}}≈97.3%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $51.7%$ of the variance,\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $80.6%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $3$ dimensions would preserve $97.3%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/cc/ccc57a7e978a8b123dd21da151c2b0fec3080be9d39f1c8f26cc3b19c0cbd2b1.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $3\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90%$ of the variance."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "780b7db4-aa11-5ca7-beff-a5f093f3ef15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528579} {:answer/id #uuid "8b461c20-1050-5d9a-91cd-8bc9ef709386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528633} {:answer/id #uuid "b8fe6545-9475-5823-b8b7-1c317fcf1b30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528752} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6031ed-ea6d-5e9d-a2e7-a3897dbc6f57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528803} :answer-field/id #uuid "0104538f-abdf-5164-878b-46a18f047fce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528937} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2646603c-69ca-578d-954e-dddbf2e2cb50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528399} {:answer/id #uuid "32fc720c-133a-5c75-a08a-0c1b2ac177b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528425} {:answer/id #uuid "e85b414d-9378-5c62-830c-20363052d009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528425} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4176f7b-81bc-51fd-bd74-a22248865cb9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529033}] :question/id #uuid "c6804c35-f8e1-5715-b170-24ee05ad3186" :question/math-academy-id "q-355842" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.42 & -0.33 & -0.4 \\\\ -0.94 & 1.07 & -0.08 \\\\ 1.11 & 0.12 & 1.71 \\\\ -0.79 & -1.51 & -0.9 \\\\ 1.04 & 0.66 & -0.32\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.244 & 0.013 & 0.022 & -0.603 & 0.759 \\\\ -0.051 & -0.763 & 0.397 & 0.405 & 0.307 \\\\ 0.671 & 0.439 & 0.394 & 0.235 & 0.383 \\\\ -0.644 & 0.452 & 0.021 & 0.57 & 0.237 \\\\ 0.272 & -0.144 & -0.828 & 0.305 & 0.355\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.730 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.751 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.210 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.617 & 0.395 & -0.68 \\\\ 0.461 & -0.882 & -0.095 \\\\ 0.638 & 0.255 & 0.727\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $85%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=2.730,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=1.751,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1.210.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.730}^{2}}{{2.730}^{2}+{1.751}^{2}+{1.210}^{2}}≈62.2%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{2.730}^{2}+{1.751}^{2}}{{2.730}^{2}+{1.751}^{2}+{1.210}^{2}}≈87.8%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $62.2%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $87.8%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/b2/b2f5cc8cae6fac8d24e20c045990d39d088a34df26402a7f1e5137070579de03.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $85%$ of the variance."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543481 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3e3a4e0-3072-52d5-8dd2-90ed5a353f4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543155} {:answer/id #uuid "c6ba0e8a-c0bf-547e-adcc-0ed6d87df774" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543158} {:answer/id #uuid "d15669cf-2800-56d9-84de-8bb935560c1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543180}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543158} :answer-field/id #uuid "20bb4000-6ffb-5bec-a89b-04e5bbaff99a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543312} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1427796d-eb86-552f-957a-f1407ab65cae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542691} {:answer/id #uuid "18b29fe7-3f3b-5d6d-8700-561ac31c65f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542702} {:answer/id #uuid "5c35ddf2-7858-53f4-9880-73848035b2d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542888} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6593be-698b-54b5-93a1-296d85cbd08f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542968} :answer-field/id #uuid "22a03984-b019-540f-8de4-513dc1ddbb0f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543314}] :question/id #uuid "1c7b66a2-e4ef-5d7b-9466-353945dad93a" :question/math-academy-id "q-355845" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-0.6 & -0.95 & -1.02 \\\\ 0.79 & 0.33 & 1.56 \\\\ -1.48 & -1.17 & -0.66 \\\\ 0.67 & 0.8 & -0.15 \\\\ 0.63 & 0.99 & 0.27\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.462 & 0.174 & -0.685 & 0.1 & 0.527 \\\\ 0.473 & -0.718 & -0.24 & 0.358 & 0.272 \\\\ -0.613 & -0.29 & 0.546 & 0.457 & 0.181 \\\\ 0.253 & 0.517 & -0.053 & 0.798 & -0.17 \\\\ 0.35 & 0.319 & 0.416 & -0.127 & 0.766\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}3.171 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.32 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.477 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.615 & 0.231 & -0.754 \\\\ 0.587 & 0.505 & 0.633 \\\\ 0.527 & -0.832 & 0.175\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $95%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=3.171,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=1.32,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=0.477.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.171}^{2}}{{3.171}^{2}+{1.32}^{2}+{0.477}^{2}}≈83.6%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.171}^{2}+{1.32}^{2}}{{3.171}^{2}+{1.32}^{2}+{0.477}^{2}}≈98.1%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $83.6%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $98.1%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/1d/1d967881aaa7c2a7cd7f537d70d3637edae36b3edf62e15ac67949925d5ee2ef.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $2\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $95%$ of the variance."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ec937d1-e5b9-5630-8e26-295f9d71c684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542894} {:answer/id #uuid "739620ba-aa1b-5b3d-8863-a2e8f0022963" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542943} {:answer/id #uuid "88e62841-c860-50af-be14-c96305a4714d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543000} {:answer/id #uuid "c408f96e-ccbc-5cb8-8e08-9511939a15b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ dimension" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ dimensions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542943} :answer-field/id #uuid "5faada09-26d3-54a9-9980-7006e9efb72b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543350} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da702c8-fb50-57a2-aba8-86d7f2e992e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the original number of features" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542716} {:answer/id #uuid "25191f9d-4f63-51c7-86f9-6e2eba8f5813" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542735} {:answer/id #uuid "54161fc7-2907-5cdd-98f5-833fcf9a93ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the eigenvalue is smaller than average" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542865} {:answer/id #uuid "7cb19297-4a76-5a65-8e56-43999bd8dc66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "if the singular value is greater than $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the amount of cumulative variance preserved" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542735} :answer-field/id #uuid "68067e86-ba42-567b-bdcd-ca00107fc431" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543353}] :question/id #uuid "6d89bf53-876c-5233-a22b-c8fa7b16fa9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-355902" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $4$ features in the form of the $5\\times 4$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0.93 & 0.98 & -1.44 & -1.13 \\\\ -0.14 & -0.96 & 0.96 & 1.58 \\\\ -1.06 & -1.09 & -0.61 & -0.05 \\\\ -0.86 & 0.96 & 0.42 & 0.08 \\\\ 1.12 & 0.1 & 0.68 & -0.48\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.725 & 0.1 & -0.166 & -0.483 & 0.452 \\\\ 0.64 & -0.269 & -0.185 & -0.532 & 0.449 \\\\ 0.207 & 0.729 & -0.311 & 0.363 & 0.444 \\\\ 0.032 & 0.06 & 0.889 & 0.076 & 0.446 \\\\ -0.149 & -0.619 & -0.227 & 0.588 & 0.444\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}3.067 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.933 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1.517 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0.763 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.384 & -0.717 & -0.539 & -0.218 \\\\ -0.5 & -0.229 & 0.78 & -0.297 \\\\ 0.471 & -0.643 & 0.31 & 0.518 \\\\ 0.617 & -0.141 & 0.06 & -0.772\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. \n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components.\n\nWe should pick components based on \n{{field-1}}.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "In reducing the dimensionality of the dataset, we aim to preserve as much of the original variance as possible. \n\nWe should pick components based on $\\text{the amount of cumulative variance preserved}.$\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\nIn our example, the singular values are\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=3.067,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=1.933,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1.517,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.763.\n$$\n\nLet us now compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.067}^{2}}{{3.067}^{2}+{1.933}^{2}+{1.517}^{2}+{0.763}^{2}}≈58.7%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.067}^{2}+{1.933}^{2}}{{3.067}^{2}+{1.933}^{2}+{1.517}^{2}+{0.763}^{2}}≈82.0%.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first three principal components is \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}{\\sigma }_{i}^{2}}=\\frac{{3.067}^{2}+{1.933}^{2}+{1.517}^{2}}{{3.067}^{2}+{1.933}^{2}+{1.517}^{2}+{0.763}^{2}}≈96.4%.\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain that\n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $58.7%$ of the variance,\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $82.0%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $3$ dimensions would preserve $96.4%$ of the variance.\n\n![](images/ff/ff1aa60e989377c3767643d2538755d932bc40669f550b57040581192a104f8c.png)\n\nTherefore, we should reduce the dataset to $3\\text{ dimensions},$ since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90%$ of the variance."}] :knowledge-point/title "Choosing the Amount of Dimensionality Reduction for a Given Dataset"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204107 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203877 :question/id #uuid "dcbf83b0-b552-52d6-97d3-9bc3ac6b0823" :question/math-academy-id "e-18844" :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $5$ datapoints with $3$ features in the form of the $5 \\times 3$ matrix\n\n![](images/c2/c2163de699b69fa804ad71bf683badb2e6e76115d88b444606f39e46eaa7aed9.svg)\n\nWe wish to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset while still preserving as much variance as possible.\n\nLet's walk through the process of reducing the dimensionality of the dataset." :question/worked-solution "We will use principal component analysis to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset. The first principal component is $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{the direction in feature space}}$ with the $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{most}}$ variance.\n\nThe second principal component is the direction $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{orthogonal}}$ to the first $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{principal component}}$ with the most variance.\n\nIn order to reduce the dimensionality of the dataset, we will project the data onto the first few principal components, picking the number of components based on the amount of cumulative variance preserved.\n\nThe first step is to $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\text{standardize the data}}.$\n\nThe standardized data matrix is\n\n![](images/15/159be3f32150b2a56f4a335990cef0c71cf6362447fc4aff521751743ab2ef58.svg)\n\nThe SVD of the standardized data matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{\\text{std}} & =UΣ{V}^{T},\\,\\text{where} \\\\ U & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.688 & 0.370 & -0.421 & 0.197 & 0.417 \\\\ 0.160 & 0.258 & 0.226 & 0.910 & -0.168 \\\\ 0.642 & 0.396 & -0.101 & -0.081 & 0.644 \\\\ 0.147 & -0.753 & -0.451 & 0.353 & 0.289 \\\\ -0.260 & -0.271 & 0.747 & 0.038 & 0.547\\end{aligned}], \\\\ Σ & =[\\begin{aligned}2.825 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.939 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0.543 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}], \\\\ V & =[\\begin{aligned}0.607 & 0.503 & -0.615 \\\\ -0.383 & 0.864 & 0.327 \\\\ -0.696 & -0.037 & -0.717\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe compute the cumulative variance preserved for different numbers of components.\n\nThe variance along a principal component is equal to the corresponding eigenvalue of the covariance matrix, which is proportional to the square of the corresponding singular value of the data matrix.\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first principal component is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2.825^2}{2.825^2 + 1.939^2 + 0.543^2} \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{66.3\\%}.\n$$\n\n- The fraction of variance preserved by projecting the data onto the first two principal components is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2.825^2 + 1.939^2}{2.825^2 + 1.939^2 + 0.543^2} \\approx \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{97.6\\%}.\n$$\n\nThus, we have that \n\n- reducing the dataset to $1$ dimension would preserve $66.3\\%$ of the variance, while\n\n- reducing the dataset to $2$ dimensions would preserve $97.6\\%$ of the variance.\n\nIf we wish to preserve $90\\%$ of the variance, we should reduce the dataset to $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{2}$ dimensions, since this is the minimum number of components that preserves at least $90\\%$ of the variance.\n\nWriting $v_1, v_2, v_3$ for the columns of $V,$ the reduced dataset is\n\n$$\nX_{\\text{red}} = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{X_{\\text{std}} \\cdot [v_1, v_2]},\n$$\n\nsince we need to project the data onto the first two principal components, which are the first two columns of $V.$\n\n**Note:** In order to invert the projection and interpret any further analysis in terms of the original features, we need to know the means and standard deviations of the original dataset $X.$ Additionally, in case we have more data, we can use the means and standard deviations of the original dataset to map the new data into the original feature space."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45f11c96-89e5-5662-81b6-06cff80a4cf2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204604 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1e3e0f1-7153-5d1e-b2fd-e5ed9412effc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the direction in feature space" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203524} {:answer/id #uuid "e5045f4c-fb9e-59fe-b2bc-e6d7604629f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the datapoint" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the direction in feature space" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203524} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ea042d6-6a31-56d0-a56c-27a81fc3e191" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-